// src/kernel/core/smp/ap_entry.pdx — R18-M1-002 / #761
//
// Placeholder AP long-mode entry point. The trampoline blob (see
// tools/ap_trampoline.S, installed at physical 0x8000 by
// ap_trampoline_install) transitions AP from real -> protected -> long
// mode, then transfers control here via an indirect absolute jump
// through the trampoline's `_ap_entry_slot` (patched at install time
// with `_ap_entry`'s higher-half kernel VA).
//
// This body is deliberately a hlt-loop: #761 lands the trampoline +
// installer mechanically and stops short of any per-CPU init so the
// review stays focused on real-mode-to-long-mode transition mechanics.
// #762 replaces this body with:
//   * per-CPU control block (cpu_local_t slot) initialization
//   * kernel GDT reload (drop the trampoline GDT)
//   * TSS load + IST setup
//   * LAPIC per-AP config (SVR, TPR, timer)
//   * scheduler enrollment (idle task registration)
//   * hand-off to sched_switch
//
// Verification (deferred to #764 boot_smp): an AP that reaches this
// halt loop under GDB will show CS = kernel code64 selector, RIP
// inside _ap_entry, CR3 = kernel PML4 — proving the trampoline
// transitioned correctly. The `cli; hlt; jmp` loop parks the AP with
// interrupts masked and no scheduler state, which is the safest
// no-op stance while #762 is pending.

module ApEntry = structure {

  pub let _ap_entry : () -> () !{sysreg} @{boot} =
    fn () -> unsafe {
      effects: { sysreg },
      capabilities: { boot },
      justification: "#761 R18-M1-002: placeholder AP long-mode entry. The trampoline (built from tools/ap_trampoline.S) jumps here via `mov ap_entry_slot(%rip), %rax; jmp *%rax` after enabling CR4.PAE, EFER.LME, CR0.PG, and reloading the trampoline's own GDT to code64/data64 selectors. Body is `cli; hlt; jmp .` — the smallest possible safe no-op that parks the AP without risking any state mutation or interrupt taking. cli guards against a stray IPI (LAPIC on this AP is not yet configured; a spurious vector would #GP into the trampoline's GDT which has no IDT — triple fault). hlt lets the core sleep; the tight `jmp` back to the halt covers wake-from-hlt without hlt-loop rearming. #762 replaces this body with per-CPU init + scheduler enrollment; the ap_entry symbol name is preserved so no caller (trampoline blob's entry-slot value) needs updating across the swap. Emits no witness line: verification lives in #764 boot_smp per-core fingerprint, which needs at minimum per-CPU logp before it can emit anything meaningful.",
      block: {
        ap_entry_halt:
          cli;
          hlt;
          jmp ap_entry_halt
      }
    }
}
